Your project targets **.NET 10**, so the best approach for `File.ReadAllText`, `File.WriteAllText`, and `Directory.Exists` is the **`System.IO.Abstractions`** NuGet package — it's battle-tested and already provides `IFileSystem` with full coverage of `File.*` and `Directory.*`.

## Setup

**1. Install the package:**
```bash
dotnet add package System.IO.Abstractions
```

**2. Register in DI (`Program.cs`):**
```csharp
builder.Services.AddSingleton<IFileSystem, FileSystem>();
```

**3. Inject `IFileSystem` into your classes:**
```csharp
using System.IO.Abstractions;

public class ConfigManager(IFileSystem fileSystem)
{
    public string Read(string path)
        => fileSystem.File.ReadAllText(path);

    public void Write(string path, string content)
        => fileSystem.File.WriteAllText(path, content);

    public bool EnsureDirectory(string path)
        => fileSystem.Directory.Exists(path);
}
```

## Testing

**Install the test helpers:**
```bash
dotnet add <YourTestProject> package System.IO.Abstractions.TestingHelpers
```

**Write tests with `MockFileSystem`:**
```csharp
using System.IO.Abstractions.TestingHelpers;

var mockFs = new MockFileSystem(new Dictionary<string, MockFileData>
{
    { "/data/config.json", new MockFileData("{\"key\": \"value\"}") }
});

var manager = new ConfigManager(mockFs);

Assert.Equal("{\"key\": \"value\"}", manager.Read("/data/config.json"));

manager.Write("/data/output.txt", "hello");
Assert.Equal("hello", mockFs.File.ReadAllText("/data/output.txt"));
```

This replaces all three statics (`File.ReadAllText`, `File.WriteAllText`, `Directory.Exists`) with a single injectable `IFileSystem` — no custom wrappers needed.
